* Graph-theoretic models\n(MIT 6.0002, lecture 3)
** Why graphs
*** Routes, rail, roads
*** Computer, financial, social networks
*** Königsberg bridges (Euler, 1736)
*** Abstraction: keep only connections
** Vocabulary
*** Nodes / vertices
*** Edges / arcs
*** Directed graph (digraph)
*** Weighted edges
*** Tree: one path between two nodes
** Representation
*** Adjacency matrix: O(V²) space, O(1) edge test
*** Adjacency list: O(V+E) space, fast neighbours
*** Node, Edge, Digraph, Graph classes
*** Graph subclasses Digraph (substitution)
** Shortest path
*** Fewest edges
*** Minimum total weight
** Depth-first search
*** Recursive, backtracking
*** Cycle avoidance: node not in path
*** Explores all simple paths (exponential)
** Breadth-first search
*** FIFO queue of paths
*** First path found has fewest edges
*** O(V+E) with a visited set
** Weighted shortest path
*** BFS answer can cost more
*** Weighted DFS with pruning
*** Priority queue: Dijkstra
@endmindmap
